Dear Hiring Team,

I am pleased to apply for the *Android Engineer* role with *LMI*. I bring more than three years of software engineering experience building secure, production applications and a 4.0 Master of Science in Computer Science from Pace University. My strongest match is the reliability work behind user-facing, asynchronous systems: at Sperse, I paired Kotlin and TypeScript application features with Temporal workflows and Redis caching so 10,000+ users could retain progress and recover cleanly from interrupted operations. That foundation maps directly to LMI's need for reliable application syncing, offline data handling, and a consistent field experience.

My work also reflects the integration and quality discipline required for an RFID mobile app. I built validated, tenant-aware interfaces exposing 160+ backend actions, then added production monitoring and GitHub Actions CI/CD with trace-based evaluations to catch latency, accuracy, and safety regressions before release. At Qualitest, I designed a modular Java storage service spanning PostgreSQL, Redis, S3, Google Cloud Storage, and MinIO, using expiring signed URLs for secure storage and supporting 100K+ asynchronous executions each week with retries and error recovery. These are transferable patterns for Jetpack Compose state, Kotlin coroutines and Flow, local caching, secure device-to-backend syncing, and troubleshooting hardware integrations such as RFID readers, barcode scanners, sensors, and Zebra SDKs.

I would bring a hands-on, learning-oriented approach to LMI's multi-module Android codebase. Across my roles, I have worked through branches, pull requests, code reviews, merge conflicts, automated testing, and CI/CD release workflows while collaborating with backend and business teams. I am eager to deepen my experience with Android SDK development, Jetpack Compose, Room, Dagger Hilt, Zebra handheld devices, cybersecurity, and application hardening within DoD environments—without losing sight of clean code, careful debugging, and production reliability.

LMI's mission to accelerate government impact with innovation and speed is compelling because this role connects engineering quality to tangible logistics outcomes. I would welcome the opportunity to help LMI deliver mission-ready technology that lets warfighters and logisticians scan tags, track assets, and keep operations moving. I can be reached at #ph or #eml. Thank you for your consideration.

